<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matthew III Csák</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1260–<a href="/years/1321">1321</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matthew III Csák was a Hungarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> who built a vast domain in the north-west of the kingdom, largely in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, as royal power collapsed around 1300. From his seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a> he ruled almost as an independent prince. He resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles I of Anjou</a>, whose victory at Rozgony in 1312 weakened his allies, and his domain broke up after his death in 1321.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matthew-iii-csak.jpg" alt="Matthew III Csák" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16th_century_portrait_of_Matthew_III_Cs%C3%A1k.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Matthew was a son of the Palatine Peter I Csák, of the Csák kindred, which medieval chroniclers traced to Szabolcs, the second captain of the Hungarians. Around 1283 he and his brother Csák inherited their father's lands at Komárom and Szenic, and their uncles' estates around Nagytapolcsány, Hrussó and Tata. A diploma records that he was lame, either from birth or from a war wound. In 1291 he joined King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-iii-of-hungary">Andrew III of Hungary</a>'s campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and the next year he recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pozsony</a> and Detrekő for the king from the rebel Nicholas I Kőszegi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Andrew made him master of the horse and ispán of Pozsony County, and in 1296 Palatine, but their relations soured and Matthew lost that office in 1297, though he went on styling himself Palatine. He enlarged his lands by purchase, pressure on neighbouring landowners and outright seizure, acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trencsén</a> around the end of 1296 and set up a court of his own around 1297, usurping royal prerogatives. After the death of Andrew, the last male <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpad">Árpád</a>, on 14 January 1301, he backed in turn the Neapolitan claimant Charles and Wenceslaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, who granted him Trencsén and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nyitra</a> counties.</p><p>In 1308 the papal legate Cardinal Gentile Portino da Montefiore persuaded him to accept Charles, who named him Palatine and then master of the treasury, yet Matthew kept away from the coronations and went on taking castles. He was excommunicated in 1311 after pillaging the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. By then his domain held 14 counties and about 50 castles. On 15 June 1312 the king's armies defeated him and the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amadeus-aba">Amade Aba</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rozgony">Battle of Rozgony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Royal and Bohemian invasions in 1314 and 1315 failed to take his domain, and he held it until his death on 18 March 1321. His son had died before him, and his cousin Stephen Sternberg lost the lands to the king within months. The region was still called Matthew's Land, terra Matthei, into the 15th century. In the 19th-century Slovak national revival, writers such as Ľudovít Štúr made him a national hero, a view Slovak historiography rejected by 1923.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matthew III Csák” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…grandson of Andrew II born in Venice, faced rival claimants backed by the papacy and Naples, and real power in many regions passed to oligarchs such as <b>Matthew Csák</b> in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in Transylvania and the Kőszegi family in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the same time much of the country was controlled by great lords, the so-called oligarchs, who ruled their regions as independent princes: <b>Matthew Csák</b> in the north-west, Amadeus Aba in the north-east, Ladislaus Kán in Transylvania and the Kőszegi family in the west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Matthew III Csák</b>, of a leading baronial clan, built up power in the Váh valley in the 1290s as royal authority collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/state/domain-of-matthew-csak">Domain of Matthew Csák</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trencin">Trenčín</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.89,18.04&amp;year=1299" aria-label="Trenčín on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4967 457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Matthew III Csák"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5417 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5417 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.89,18.04&amp;year=1299">What was here in 1299? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
